var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"mpc_module/#Tracking-MPC-With-EOptInterface","page":"MPC Module","text":"This page explains the ModelingToolkit-to-JuMP MPC workflow used by the examples in this repository. It is written as a reproducible research note: what model is used, what the controller changes, what output is tracked, and what gets updated at each sampling time.\n\nThis page describes the MPC research branch, which uses ModelingToolkit 10. It is separate from the released main branch and should be cited with its branch name and commit hash.","title":"Tracking MPC With EOptInterface"},{"category":"section","location":"mpc_module/#Basic-Workflow","page":"MPC Module","text":"For a mechanistic plant model, the normal workflow is:\n\nwrite the plant in ModelingToolkit;\nchoose manipulated variables, such as aeration or flow rate;\nchoose tracked outputs and setpoints;\nbuild one JuMP MPC problem;\nduring simulation, update the current state and solve again.\n\nThe main functions are:\n\nbuild_tracking_mpc(...): build the JuMP prediction and tracking problem;\nsolve_tracking_mpc!(...): update the current state, solve, and read back the first control move;\nupdate_stage_parameter!(...): update a disturbance or feed preview;\nupdate_tracking_targets!(...): change setpoints or soft bounds during a run.\n\nThe DMC step-response path is separate and starts from register_dmcsystem(...).","title":"Basic Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"mpc_module/#Minimal-Example","page":"MPC Module","text":"using EOptInterface\nusing ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\nusing JuMP, Ipopt\n\n@parameters u = 0.0 d = 1.0\n@variables x(t)\n@named sys = ODESystem([D(x) ~ -0.8 * x + u + d], t, [x], [u, d])\n\nmodel = Model(Ipopt.Optimizer)\nset_silent(model)\n\ncfg = TrackingMPCConfig(\n    PH = 5,\n    CH = 2,\n    dt = 1.0,\n    integrator = \"IE\",\n    system_kind = :ode,\n    state_lower = -5.0,\n    state_upper = 5.0,\n)\n\nctrl = build_tracking_mpc(\n    model,\n    sys;\n    control_specs = [\n        MPCControlSpec(\n            sym = sys.u,\n            lower = 0.0,\n            upper = 2.0,\n            delta_max = 0.4,\n            move_weight = 0.1,\n            first_move_weight = 1.0,\n        ),\n    ],\n    output_specs = [\n        MPCOutputSpec(\n            sym = sys.x,\n            setpoint = 1.0,\n            track_weight = 1.0,\n            terminal_weight = 2.0,\n            lower_soft = 0.9,\n            upper_soft = 1.1,\n            slack_weight = 100.0,\n        ),\n    ],\n    stage_param_defaults = Dict(sys.d => fill(1.0, cfg.PH + 1)),\n    config = cfg,\n)\n\nresult = solve_tracking_mpc!(ctrl, Dict(sys.x => 0.0), Dict(sys.u => 0.0))\nu_apply = result.controls[sys.u][1]\n\nresult.controls contains the optimized input trajectory. result.predictions contains the predicted states. result.metrics contains objective terms that are useful for reports.","title":"Minimal Example"},{"category":"section","location":"mpc_module/#Meaning-of-the-Main-Settings","page":"MPC Module","text":"MPCControlSpec describes one manipulated variable.\n\nsym: the ModelingToolkit symbol used as the control input;\nlower, upper: hard bounds;\ndelta_max: maximum move between samples;\nmove_weight: penalty on input movement over the horizon;\nfirst_move_weight: penalty on changing from the previously applied input.\n\nMPCOutputSpec describes one tracked output.\n\nsym: state or algebraic quantity to track;\nsetpoint: target value;\ntrack_weight: tracking-error weight;\nterminal_weight: extra weight on the final predicted point;\nlower_soft, upper_soft, slack_weight: optional soft operating zone.\n\nTrackingMPCConfig holds the numerical settings.\n\nPH: prediction horizon length;\nCH: number of free control moves;\ndt: sample time;\nintegrator: transcription rule, such as \"IE\" or \"RK4\";\nsystem_kind: :ode or :dae;\nstate_lower, state_upper: default state bounds.","title":"Meaning of the Main Settings"},{"category":"section","location":"mpc_module/#Closed-Loop-Use","page":"MPC Module","text":"Inside a simulation callback or a hand-written loop, the repeated step is:\n\nstate_values = current_state_map(integ, sys)\nupdate_stage_parameter!(ctrl, sys.d, disturbance_forecast)\nupdate_tracking_targets!(ctrl; setpoints = Dict(sys.x => 1.2))\nresult = solve_tracking_mpc!(ctrl, state_values, Dict(sys.u => integ.ps[sys.u]))\nu_apply = result.controls[sys.u][1]\n\nThe JuMP model is not rebuilt at each sample. Only the current state, previously applied input, setpoints, and previews are updated.\n\nFor a simple live line while the controller runs:\n\nresult = solve_tracking_mpc!(\n    ctrl,\n    state_values,\n    previous_controls;\n    show_status = true,\n)\n\nThe NDMC notebook adds its own status line with the variables used in that case. That extra printing is part of the example, not a different controller.","title":"Closed-Loop Use"},{"category":"section","location":"mpc_module/#DAE-and-DMC-Notes","page":"MPC Module","text":"Use TrackingMPCConfig(system_kind = :dae) when the prediction model includes algebraic equations that should remain in the optimization problem. The small DAE example is examples/dae_registration_demo.jl.\n\nUse register_dmcsystem(...) when the plant has already been reduced to step responses and a classic DMC formulation is enough. The small DMC example is examples/dmc_registration_demo.jl.","title":"DAE and DMC Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"mpc_module/#Suggested-Reading-Order","page":"MPC Module","text":"For the tracking-MPC path:\n\nexamples/tracking_mpc_demo.jl;\nthis page;\nsrc/trackingmpc.jl;\nexamples/ndmc_conductivity_mpc_demo.jl or the NDMC notebook.\n\nThe NDMC case now has one repository implementation: examples/NDMCExample.jl loads the model and simulation code from examples/ndmc_case.jl, optional timing code from examples/ndmc_profile.jl, and plotting code from examples/ndmc_plots.jl. The repository notebook, notebooks/ndmc_conductivity_mpc_simple.ipynb, runs the same case.\n\nThe canonical NDMC run uses a 20 s MPC sample time and saves the plant trajectory every 10 s over 0-4000 s. Versioned comparison CSVs use the same 10 s output grid. Timing tables are machine dependent and are not versioned.\n\nFor debugging a failed solve, the helper routines in src/mpcutils.jl can print constraints, check initial-condition synchronization, and record MPC predictions. They are supporting tools, not the main controller definition.","title":"Suggested Reading Order"},{"category":"section","location":"#EOptInterface","page":"Home","text":"Documentation for the MPC research branch of EOptInterface.","title":"EOptInterface"},{"category":"section","location":"#Guides","page":"Home","text":"MPC Module","title":"Guides"},{"category":"section","location":"#Scope","page":"Home","text":"EOptInterface connects ModelingToolkit models to JuMP optimization models. The examples focus on small algebraic/ODE/DAE registrations and reproducible MPC/DMC runs drawn from wastewater and process-control case studies.\n\nPages = [\"index.md\", \"mpc_module.md\"]\nDepth = 2\n\n","title":"Scope"},{"category":"type","location":"#EOptInterface.DMCRegistration","page":"Home","text":"DMCRegistration\n\nContainer returned by register_dmcsystem(...).\n\nIt stores the step-response data, the JuMP variables, and the prediction constraints.\n\nThink of this object as the DMC counterpart of TrackingMPCController. It is the object that keeps all DMC-related JuMP references together so later code can update data, inspect predictions, or build an objective on top of the prediction model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.DMCRegistration"},{"category":"type","location":"#EOptInterface.MPCControlSpec","page":"Home","text":"MPCControlSpec\n\nOne manipulated input in the MPC problem.\n\nFor example, this could be an aeration rate, recycle flow, or any model parameter that the controller is allowed to change. The fields give its bounds, move limit, and move penalty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.MPCControlSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"#EOptInterface.MPCLog","page":"Home","text":"MPCLog\n\nSimple container for closed-loop MPC logging.\n\nIt stores:\n\nstate indices,\nlogged times,\nstate histories,\ncontrol histories,\nprediction times,\nprediction histories,\nand scalar metric histories.\n\nThink of this as the package's \"black box recorder\" for one MPC run. It does not solve anything by itself. It simply keeps all the time histories in one place so later code can inspect, plot, or compare them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.MPCLog"},{"category":"type","location":"#EOptInterface.MPCOutputSpec","page":"Home","text":"MPCOutputSpec\n\nOne output that the MPC tries to keep near a target.\n\nThe output can have a setpoint and optional soft lower/upper limits. Soft limits are penalized through slack_weight instead of making the solve fail immediately.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.MPCOutputSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"#EOptInterface.TrackingMPCConfig","page":"Home","text":"TrackingMPCConfig\n\nBasic numerical settings for one tracking MPC experiment.\n\nPH is the prediction horizon, CH is the number of free control moves, and dt is the sample time used in the prediction model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.TrackingMPCConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"#EOptInterface.TrackingMPCController","page":"Home","text":"TrackingMPCController\n\nStored MPC problem used during closed-loop simulation.\n\nUsers normally create this with build_tracking_mpc(...) and then pass it to solve_tracking_mpc!(...) at each sampling time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.TrackingMPCController"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._affexpr_var_coeff_pairs-Tuple{JuMP.AffExpr}","page":"Home","text":"_affexpr_var_coeff_pairs(func)\n\nExtract (variable, coefficient) pairs from a JuMP.AffExpr.\n\nThis is used by the constraint-audit functions later in this file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._affexpr_var_coeff_pairs"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._apply_value_clips-Tuple{Real}","page":"Home","text":"_apply_value_clips(value; lower_clip=nothing, upper_clip=nothing)\n\nClip one numeric value to optional lower and upper limits.\n\nThis is used when the current plant state is copied into MPC initial conditions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._apply_value_clips"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._bound_for_var-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"build_state_trajs_from_vars!(model, sys, vars, N; lb=0.0, ub=1e6, rhs0=0.0, store_ext=true)\n\nCreate one JuMP state trajectory for each variable in vars. Returns (x_vars, c_ic) where:\n\nx_vars[var] is the trajectory vector for the MTK variable or resolved leaf object.\nc_ic[var] is the initial-condition equality on the first time point.\n\nlb, ub, and rhs0 may be scalars or dictionaries keyed by the MTK state variables. Top-level state variables are supported in addition to nested subsystem variables.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\nRead each MTK state name and split it into a subsystem path and a leaf state name.\nGroup states by subsystem so naming and lookup stay consistent.\nFor each state, choose the lower bound, upper bound, and IC right-hand side.\nCreate one JuMP vector of length N for that state.\nAdd one equality constraint to pin the first time point to the chosen IC.\nStore the trajectories and IC constraints in dictionaries keyed by the MTK state symbol.\nOptionally save those dictionaries in model.ext for later MPC helpers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._bound_for_var"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._canonical_bound_spec-Tuple{Any, Any, Symbol}","page":"Home","text":"_canonical_bound_spec(sys, spec, pool)\n\nNormalize bound dictionaries onto the standard MTK keys.\n\nIf spec is not a dictionary, it is returned unchanged. This is used when build_tracking_mpc(...) receives state bounds or initial values keyed by user-facing symbols.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._canonical_bound_spec"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._canonical_parameter_var_dict-Tuple{Any, AbstractDict}","page":"Home","text":"_canonical_parameter_var_dict(sys, data)\n\nRewrite a dictionary so all keys are the standard parameter symbols from parameters(sys).\n\nThis matters because users may pass different but equivalent MTK names. The rest of the package wants one consistent key form before it writes JuMP variables into the MPC model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._canonical_parameter_var_dict"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._canonical_state_var_dict-Tuple{Any, AbstractDict}","page":"Home","text":"_canonical_state_var_dict(sys, data)\n\nRewrite a dictionary so all keys are the standard state symbols from unknowns(sys).\n\nThis is the state-side companion to _canonical_parameter_var_dict(...). It keeps later IC and trajectory updates keyed by one canonical MTK symbol set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._canonical_state_var_dict"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._canonical_value_map-Tuple{Any, AbstractDict, Symbol}","page":"Home","text":"_canonical_value_map(sys, values, pool)\n\nConvert an input dictionary to a Num => Float64 map with standard MTK keys.\n\nThis is used before writing current plant values or previous controls into the MPC model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._canonical_value_map"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._constraint_uses_any_var-Tuple{Any, Set, Set{String}}","page":"Home","text":"_constraint_uses_any_var(func, target_idxs, target_names)\n\nCheck whether a JuMP/MOI expression contains one of the variables of interest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._constraint_uses_any_var"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._format_tracking_scalar-Tuple{Real}","page":"Home","text":"_format_tracking_scalar(value; digits=3)\n\nFormat one number for status printing.\n\nIt prints NaN cleanly and rounds ordinary numbers to a small number of digits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._format_tracking_scalar"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._metric_key-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"_metric_key(prefix, label)\n\nBuild a metric key for the metrics dictionary returned by solve_tracking_mpc!(...).\n\nExamples are keys such as :track_C1_y or :move_Q_air_u.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._metric_key"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._register_tracking_system!-Tuple{TrackingMPCConfig, JuMP.Model, Any, Vector{Symbolics.Num}, Dict{Symbolics.Num, Vector{JuMP.VariableRef}}, AbstractDict, JuMP.VariableRef}","page":"Home","text":"_register_tracking_system!(cfg, model, sys, p_disc, p_disc_vars, x_vars, t_stage)\n\nAdd the ODE or DAE prediction equations to the JuMP model.\n\nIt calls:\n\nregister_odesystem(...) when system_kind = :ode\nregister_daesystem(...) when system_kind = :dae\n\nbuild_tracking_mpc(...) calls this after the state, control, and stage trajectories are in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._register_tracking_system!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._resolve_path-Tuple{Any, Vector{Symbol}}","page":"Home","text":"_resolve_path(sys, path_syms)\n\nWalk through nested subsystem names and return the final object.\n\nThis is an internal helper for flowsheet-style MTK models with nested subsystems.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._resolve_path"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._resolve_tracking_query-Tuple{TrackingMPCController, Any, Symbol}","page":"Home","text":"_resolve_tracking_query(ctrl, query, kind)\n\nConvert a state, control, or stage-parameter query into one standard MTK symbol.\n\nIt turns a user query into one standard MTK symbol. The query can be:\n\nan MTK symbol,\na display name,\nor a JuMP base name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._resolve_tracking_query"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._resolved_base_name-Tuple{Any, Union{Nothing, String}, AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"_resolved_base_name(sym, custom, suffix)\n\nBuild the JuMP base name for one MPC variable family.\n\nThe code uses the same naming rule for controls, outputs, and stage parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._resolved_base_name"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._root_symbol_prefix-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"_root_symbol_prefix(sys)\n\nReturn the MTK root-name prefix for a system.\n\nFor example, if the system name is sys, this function returns \"sys₊\". It is used when the package tries to match sys.x with the unprefixed symbol x(t).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._root_symbol_prefix"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._single_var_from_affexpr-Tuple{JuMP.AffExpr}","page":"Home","text":"_single_var_from_affexpr(func)\n\nReturn the one variable term from an affine expression when it has exactly one.\n\nIf an affine expression contains exactly one variable term, this returns that variable and its coefficient. Otherwise it returns (nothing, 0.0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._single_var_from_affexpr"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._split_path_and_state-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"_split_path_and_state(var)\n\nReturn the subsystem path and terminal state name for one variable.\n\nThis keeps the path-handling code short in the functions below.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._split_path_and_state"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._stage_values-Tuple{Any, Int64, AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"_stage_values(values, N, label)\n\nTurn one stage-parameter input into a length-N vector.\n\nIt accepts scalars, tuples, and vectors. This is used by build_tracking_mpc(...) and update_stage_parameter!(...).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._stage_values"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._store_result_alias!-Tuple{AbstractDict, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"_store_result_alias!(dest, original, canonical, value)\n\nStore one result under both the user-facing key and the standard key.\n\nThis makes the returned controls and predictions dictionaries easier to use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._store_result_alias!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._subsystem_path_syms-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"_subsystem_path_syms(var)\n\nReturn only the subsystem-path part of an MTK variable name.\n\nFor clarifier₊outlet_stream₊S_N2O(t), this returns the path symbols and drops the final state name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._subsystem_path_syms"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._system_symbol_aliases-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"_system_symbol_aliases(sys, sym)\n\nBuild a list of valid string aliases for one MTK symbol.\n\nThis lets the code accept both prefixed and unprefixed MTK names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._system_symbol_aliases"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._system_symbol_lookup-Tuple{Any, Symbol}","page":"Home","text":"_system_symbol_lookup(sys, pool)\n\nBuild the string-to-symbol lookup table used by canonical_system_symbol(...).\n\npool decides whether the lookup is built from unknowns, parameters, or both.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._system_symbol_lookup"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._time_index-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}, Real}","page":"Home","text":"_time_index(times, target; match=:nearest, atol=1e-8)\n\nFind the index of a target time in a logged time vector.\n\nUse match=:nearest for the closest entry or match=:exact when you want the time to match within a tolerance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._time_index"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._tracking_aliases-Tuple{TrackingMPCController, Symbolics.Num, AbstractVector{<:JuMP.VariableRef}, Symbol}","page":"Home","text":"_tracking_aliases(ctrl, sym, traj, kind)\n\nBuild the list of names that can refer to one trajectory.\n\nIt includes MTK names, display names, sanitized names, and the stored public base names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._tracking_aliases"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._tracking_selected_syms-Tuple{TrackingMPCController, Any, Symbol}","page":"Home","text":"_tracking_selected_syms(ctrl, queries, kind)\n\nChoose which output or control symbols appear in print_tracking_status(...).\n\nIt decides which output or control symbols should appear in the printed status line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._tracking_selected_syms"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._tracking_state_snapshot-Tuple{TrackingMPCController, Union{Nothing, AbstractDict}}","page":"Home","text":"_tracking_state_snapshot(ctrl, state_values)\n\nNormalize one state => value dictionary onto the standard MTK state keys.\n\nprint_tracking_status(...) uses this so it can show the current plant state with the same symbols used inside the controller.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._tracking_state_snapshot"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._trajectory_base_name-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:JuMP.VariableRef}}","page":"Home","text":"_trajectory_base_name(traj)\n\nReturn the base JuMP name for one trajectory vector.\n\nIf the first variable is named x[1], this returns x. This is used by the trajectory query helpers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._trajectory_base_name"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._validate_unique_syms-Tuple{Any, Any, Symbol, AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"_validate_unique_syms(sys, specs, pool, label)\n\nCheck whether a list of control or output specs contains duplicate MTK symbols.\n\nbuild_tracking_mpc(...) uses this before any JuMP variables are created.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._validate_unique_syms"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._value_affine-Tuple{JuMP.Model, MathOptInterface.ScalarAffineFunction{Float64}}","page":"Home","text":"_value_affine(model, func)\n\nInternal fallback evaluator for MOI.ScalarAffineFunction.\n\nThis is used when JuMP.value(func) is not available for a particular affine function object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._value_affine"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface._value_or_float-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"_value_or_float(x)\n\nReturn a plain Float64.\n\nIf x is already numeric, it is converted directly. If x is a JuMP expression, the helper reads its current value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface._value_or_float"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.build_tracking_mpc-Tuple{JuMP.Model, Any}","page":"Home","text":"build_tracking_mpc(model, sys; control_specs, output_specs, config=..., stage_param_defaults=...)\n\nBuild one tracking MPC problem around a ModelingToolkit ODE or DAE system.\n\nThis function builds the JuMP model once. It adds state trajectories, control trajectories, optional preview parameters, tracking terms, and move penalties.\n\nThis is the main setup function for the tracking MPC examples.\n\nWhat this function creates:\n\none JuMP trajectory for each model state;\none JuMP trajectory for each control input;\noptional stage-wise preview trajectories, such as disturbances or prices;\none objective made of tracking penalties, soft-bound penalties, and move penalties;\none TrackingMPCController value that stores the JuMP references needed during simulation.\n\nWhy this matters during simulation:\n\nthe expensive model construction happens once here;\nlater online MPC steps only update values and re-solve;\nthis avoids rebuilding the full JuMP model at every sample.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\nCheck horizon settings, control specifications, and output specifications.\nNormalize all control, output, and stage-parameter names to one MTK key.\nBuild or reuse the state trajectories and IC constraints.\nCreate one JuMP trajectory for each control and each stage parameter.\nCall _register_tracking_system!(...) to add the ODE or DAE prediction equations.\nBuild the tracking terms, soft-bound terms, and move penalties.\nStore the resulting JuMP references and naming maps in a TrackingMPCController so later online solves only update values and re-optimize.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.build_tracking_mpc"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.canonical_system_parameter-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"canonical_system_parameter(sys, sym; allow_missing=false)\n\nResolve sym to the standard parameter symbol in parameters(sys).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.canonical_system_parameter"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.canonical_system_symbol-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"canonical_system_symbol(sys, sym; pool=:all, allow_missing=false)\n\nResolve a user-facing MTK symbol such as sys.x or sys.u to the standard MTK symbol used inside the package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.canonical_system_symbol"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.canonical_system_unknown-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"canonical_system_unknown(sys, sym; allow_missing=false)\n\nResolve sym to the standard state symbol in unknowns(sys).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.canonical_system_unknown"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.canonicalize_system_symbols-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"canonicalize_system_symbols(sys, syms; pool=:all, allow_missing=false)\n\nNormalize a list of system symbols. Keep the first-seen order. Drop duplicates after normalization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.canonicalize_system_symbols"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.check_ic_sync-Tuple{AbstractDict, AbstractDict}","page":"Home","text":"check_ic_sync(current_values, ic_constraints; atol=1e-8)\n\nCompare a dictionary of current values against the RHS stored in initial-condition constraints. Returns a named tuple with the maximum absolute error and all per-key differences.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.check_ic_sync"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.collect_subsystems-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"collect_subsystems(sys, vars; skip_invalid=true)\n\nResolve the subsystem path referenced by each variable in vars and return the deduplicated subsystem objects in order of first appearance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.collect_subsystems"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.compute_step_prediction_errors-Tuple{MPCLog, Any}","page":"Home","text":"compute_step_prediction_errors(logctx, key; actual_key=key, step_index=2, match=:nearest, atol=1e-8)\n\nCompare one predicted step against the later logged actual value. Return times, predicted values, actual values, and errors.\n\nThis helper is useful after a closed-loop run is finished. It answers a simple question: \"When the MPC predicted the plant state several steps ahead, how wrong was that prediction when the future actually arrived?\"\n\nExample:\n\nat solve time t_k, the MPC predicts x[k+1], x[k+2], and so on;\nlater, the plant actually reaches t_{k+1}, t_{k+2}, and so on;\nthis helper lines up those two records and computes the difference.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nrecord_mpc_prediction!(...) must already have stored the prediction histories;\nlog_mpc_state!(...) must already have stored the actual plant histories;\nthis function reads both logs and returns aligned arrays for later plotting or statistics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.compute_step_prediction_errors"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.control_traj-Tuple{TrackingMPCController, Any}","page":"Home","text":"control_traj(ctrl, query)\n\nReturn the JuMP control trajectory that matches query.\n\nYou can query by MTK symbol, display name, or JuMP base name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.control_traj"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.current_state_map-Tuple{Any, AbstractDict}","page":"Home","text":"current_state_map(integ, i_state)\n\nRead the current DifferentialEquations state into a var => value dictionary.\n\nThis method uses a precomputed state => index map.\n\nUse this when you already know the mapping from each MTK state to the matching position in integ.u. This is slightly more efficient than rebuilding the mapping every time.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nmake_state_index_map(...) builds the state => index map;\nprepare_tracking_mpc_step!(...) and other online helpers often need the dictionary returned by this function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.current_state_map"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.current_state_map-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"current_state_map(integ, sys)\n\nRead the current DifferentialEquations state into a var => value dictionary.\n\nThis method builds the lookup from unknowns(sys).\n\nThis is the more convenient version of current_state_map(...). It is easier to use in quick scripts because you only need the integrator and the MTK system. The tradeoff is that it rebuilds the lookup map each time you call it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.current_state_map"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.decision_vars-Tuple{ModelingToolkit.System}","page":"Home","text":"decision_vars(sys)\ndecision_vars(sys, ps; model=nothing, horizon=nothing, build_state_trajs=false, ...)\n\nIdentify the ModelingToolkit symbols that should participate in an MPC or other dynamic optimization problem.\n\nWhat this function returns depends on build_state_trajs:\n\nbuild_state_trajs=false: returns a flat vector of symbols, consisting of\nall MTK states (unknowns(sys)), and\nthe parameters in ps, plus any parameters without defaults.\nbuild_state_trajs=true: also creates one JuMP trajectory for every state and returns a named tuple with all, states, params, x_vars, and c_ic.\n\nWhy this function exists:\n\nit gives the package one place to decide which MTK symbols go into the NLP;\nit normalizes symbols, so callers can use either raw MTK symbols or names like sys.x;\nit can also build the state trajectories used by the ODE and DAE functions.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nbuild_tracking_mpc(...) calls this to get x_vars and c_ic;\nregister_odesystem(...) and register_daesystem(...) use those trajectories;\nfull_solutions(...) uses the same symbol order when it reads values back.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\nRead all MTK states from unknowns(sys).\nNormalize the user-supplied parameter names in ps.\nAdd any parameters that have no default value, because the NLP still needs them.\nIf build_state_trajs=false, return the final symbol list.\nIf build_state_trajs=true, call build_state_trajs_from_vars!(...) to allocate one JuMP trajectory and one IC constraint per state.\nReturn the symbol list together with the trajectory containers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.decision_vars"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.default_mpc_accepted_statuses-Tuple{}","page":"Home","text":"default_mpc_accepted_statuses()\n\nReturn the default JuMP/MOI solve statuses treated as acceptable for MPC.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.default_mpc_accepted_statuses"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.display_mpc_name-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"display_mpc_name(sym)\n\nConvert an MTK symbol into a dotted display name.\n\nExamples:\n\nclarifier₊outlet_stream₊S_N2O(t) -> \"clarifier.outlet_stream.S_N2O\"\nx(t) -> \"x\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.display_mpc_name"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.dump_all_constraints-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"Home","text":"dump_all_constraints(model)\n\nPrint all JuMP constraints in a model.\n\nThis is a debugging aid for small models, not part of the normal MPC workflow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.dump_all_constraints"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.dump_single_var_affines-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"Home","text":"dump_single_var_affines(model; atol=1e-8)\n\nList single-variable affine equalities and bounds grouped by variable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.dump_single_var_affines"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.ensure_ic!-Tuple{JuMP.Model, Dict{Any, JuMP.ConstraintRef}, Dict, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"ensure_ic!(model, ic_map, x_vars, u, val)\n\nEnsure that the first point of a state trajectory has the chosen value.\n\nThis function either updates an existing initial-condition constraint or creates it.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\ndecision_vars(...; build_state_trajs=true) and build_state_trajs_from_vars!(...) create the initial-condition constraints stored in c_ic;\nprepare_tracking_mpc_step!(...) updates those constraints before each solve;\nget_ic_constraint!(...) checks that the IC constraint is present.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.ensure_ic!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.find_conflicts-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"Home","text":"find_conflicts(model; atol=1e-8)\n\nCheck a JuMP model for contradictory single-variable affine equalities and bounds. Returns a vector of human-readable problem strings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.find_conflicts"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.find_constraints_with_vars-Tuple{JuMP.Model, AbstractVector{<:JuMP.VariableRef}}","page":"Home","text":"find_constraints_with_vars(model, vars; constraint_sets=(MOI.EqualTo{Float64},), verbose=true)\n\nReturn constraints that contain one of the variables in vars.\n\nThis is useful when a state or control appears to be fixed by an unexpected constraint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.find_constraints_with_vars"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.full_solutions-Tuple{JuMP.Model, ModelingToolkit.System}","page":"Home","text":"full_solutions(model, sys)\nfull_solutions(model, sys, ps)\n\nRead solved JuMP values back into MTK symbols.\n\nThis is a reporting helper. It substitutes solved values into the MTK model and returns the observed MTK expressions.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\ndecision_vars(...) determines the symbol ordering used here;\nit is useful after register_nlsystem(...), register_odesystem(...), or register_daesystem(...);\nit lets case-study scripts stay close to the original MTK notation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.full_solutions"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.get_ic_constraint!-Tuple{JuMP.Model, AbstractVector{<:JuMP.VariableRef}}","page":"Home","text":"get_ic_constraint!(model, x; idx=1, rhs_if_missing=nothing)\n\nReturn the equality constraint for the first point of a trajectory.\n\nThis helper looks for an equality on x[idx]. It returns the constraint if it exists. It can create the constraint if it is missing. It can also delete duplicates.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nbuild_state_trajs_from_vars!(...) creates the original IC constraints;\nensure_ic!(...) updates or adds them during online MPC use;\nthis helper is mainly for checking and repair.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.get_ic_constraint!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.group_state_symbols-Tuple{Vector}","page":"Home","text":"group_state_symbols(vars)\n\nGroup variables by their leading subsystem name. For example, reactor2₊S_NO3(t) is grouped under :reactor2 with state :S_NO3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.group_state_symbols"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.is_accepted_mpc_status-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"is_accepted_mpc_status(status; accepted_statuses=default_mpc_accepted_statuses())\n\nReturn true when status is in the accepted MPC status set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.is_accepted_mpc_status"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.log_mpc_state!-Tuple{MPCLog, Any}","page":"Home","text":"log_mpc_state!(logctx, integ; control_values=Dict(), missing_control=:error)\n\nRecord the current time, states, and control values.\n\nThis is the main \"plant-side\" logging function. It reads the current DifferentialEquations integrator state and appends one new record to the log.\n\nTypical use in a closed-loop script:\n\nthe plant integrator advances in time;\nthe MPC callback computes or updates the control signal;\nthis function stores the new plant state and the applied control.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\ncurrent_state_map(...) can build a state => value dictionary from the same integrator;\nseed_mpc_log!(...) writes the initial entry before the loop starts;\nthis function then appends one more entry at each later time step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.log_mpc_state!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.make_mpc_log-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Home","text":"make_mpc_log(state_vars; control_keys=Any[], predicted_keys=Any[], metric_keys=Symbol[])\n\nCreate a generic closed-loop MPC log.\n\nUse this when you already know which states, controls, predicted channels, and metrics you want to record.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nlog_mpc_state!(...) appends measured plant data into this object;\nrecord_mpc_prediction!(...) appends the prediction from each MPC solve;\nrecord_mpc_metrics!(...) appends scalar objective terms and diagnostics;\ncompute_step_prediction_errors(...) later reads this log to compare plant reality against earlier MPC predictions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.make_mpc_log"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.make_mpc_log-Tuple{ModelingToolkit.System}","page":"Home","text":"make_mpc_log(sys; kwargs...)\n\nConvenience method that builds MPCLog directly from unknowns(sys).\n\nUse this when you already have a ModelingToolkit system and do not want to manually pass the state vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.make_mpc_log"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.make_state_index_map-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"make_state_index_map(state_vars)\n\nBuild a state -> index lookup from state_vars.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.make_state_index_map"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.mtk_generate_model_equations-Tuple{ModelingToolkit.System}","page":"Home","text":"mtk_generate_model_equations(sys)\n\nBuild callable Julia functions for each dynamic equation in a ModelingToolkit.System.\n\nMost users will not call this directly. It is useful when the raw residual functions are needed for inspection or a custom experiment. Its job is:\n\nexpand the MTK equations,\nmove each equation into residual form rhs - lhs,\nsubstitute default parameter values,\nbuild one executable function per equation.\n\nThe returned vector is useful when you want to inspect or reuse the raw model equations outside the standard tracking MPC workflow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.mtk_generate_model_equations"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.mtk_generate_reduced_expression-Tuple{Symbolics.Num, ModelingToolkit.System}","page":"Home","text":"mtk_generate_reduced_expression(expr, sys)\n\nSubstitute defaults and observed-variable definitions into one symbolic expression, then build an executable Julia function.\n\nUse this helper when you want to evaluate one derived quantity from a ModelingToolkit system without manually expanding all observed equations yourself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.mtk_generate_reduced_expression"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.objective_value_or_nan-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"Home","text":"objective_value_or_nan(model)\n\nReturn the JuMP objective value as Float64. Return NaN if the objective is not available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.objective_value_or_nan"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.prepare_tracking_mpc_step!-Tuple{TrackingMPCController, AbstractDict, AbstractDict}","page":"Home","text":"prepare_tracking_mpc_step!(ctrl, state_values, previous_controls; ...)\n\nUpdate the ICs, warm starts, and first-move bounds before solving.\n\nThis is the last setup step right before one online MPC solve. It does not solve the optimization problem by itself. Its only job is to make sure the already-built JuMP model matches the newest plant information.\n\nInputs:\n\nstate_values: the current plant state estimate or measurement;\nprevious_controls: the control values that are currently active at the plant.\n\nWhy both are needed:\n\nthe plant state tells the optimizer where the prediction starts;\nthe previous control tells the optimizer what \"next move\" is allowed under move-rate limits.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\nNormalize the incoming plant state and previous control names.\nClean very small values so the MPC starts from a numerically tidy point.\nWrite the current plant state into the first point of each state trajectory.\nFill the rest of each state trajectory with a warm start.\nUpdate the first-move anchor to the last applied control value.\nShift the previous optimal control sequence when possible.\nReapply control bounds and first-move delta limits before the next solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.prepare_tracking_mpc_step!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.pretty_subsystems-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"pretty_subsystems(sys, subs)\n\nFormat subsystem objects as human-readable sys.a.b strings when the subsystem exposes a name field. Falls back to string(obj) otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.pretty_subsystems"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.print_conflicts-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"Home","text":"print_conflicts(model; atol=1e-8)\n\nPretty-print the output of find_conflicts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.print_conflicts"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.print_tracking_status-Tuple{IO, TrackingMPCController, Any}","page":"Home","text":"print_tracking_status(io, ctrl, result; ...)\n\nPrint one short line that summarizes the current MPC solve.\n\nThe line can include time, solve status, objective value, tracked outputs, and the first control move.\n\nThis function exists for users who want live feedback while the simulation is running. It is not part of the optimization itself. It is only a reporting helper.\n\nWhat it prints:\n\nthe current time, if the caller supplies it;\nthe JuMP termination status;\nwhether that status is treated as acceptable for MPC;\nthe total objective value;\nselected tracked outputs together with their setpoints;\nselected control values, usually the first move to be applied.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nsolve_tracking_mpc!(...) can call this automatically with show_status=true;\nstate_traj(...), control_traj(...), and the naming helpers make it easier to choose which variables should appear in the printed line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.print_tracking_status"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.record_mpc_metrics!-Tuple{MPCLog, AbstractDict{<:Symbol, <:Real}}","page":"Home","text":"record_mpc_metrics!(logctx, metrics; missing=:error)\n\nAppend scalar MPC diagnostics such as tracking cost or move cost.\n\nUse this when you want more than just states and controls. For example, you may want to record:\n\nthe full objective value;\nthe tracking penalty;\nthe soft-constraint penalty;\nthe control-move penalty.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nsolve_tracking_mpc!(...) already builds a metrics dictionary;\nthis function appends those scalar values into the log;\nlater analysis scripts can plot how each objective component changed over time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.record_mpc_metrics!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.record_mpc_prediction!-Tuple{MPCLog, Real, AbstractDict}","page":"Home","text":"record_mpc_prediction!(logctx, t, predictions; missing=:error)\n\nStore the prediction trajectories from one MPC solve at time t.\n\nThis is the \"optimizer-side\" logging function. Unlike log_mpc_state!(...), which stores what the plant actually did, this function stores what the MPC predicted at one solve time.\n\nWhy this matters:\n\nafter the run, you can ask whether the MPC predictions were accurate;\nyou can compare the predicted next step with the later measured plant value;\nthat makes debugging much easier.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nsolve_tracking_mpc!(...) returns a predictions dictionary;\nthis function stores that dictionary in MPCLog;\ncompute_step_prediction_errors(...) later compares those stored predictions against the logged plant state history.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.record_mpc_prediction!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.register_daesystem-Tuple{JuMP.Model, ModelingToolkit.System, Tuple{Real, Real}, Real, String}","page":"Home","text":"register_daesystem(model, sys, tspan, tstep, integrator; ...)\n\nRegister a ModelingToolkit DAE system as JuMP constraints over time.\n\nCompared with register_odesystem(...), this function also keeps the algebraic equations in the model.\n\nAssumed equation forms:\n\nD(x) ~ f(...)\n0 ~ g(...)\nz ~ h(...) (converted internally to a residual equation)\n\nSupported integrators for the differential part:\n\n\"EE\": Explicit Euler\n\"IE\": Implicit Euler\n\"RK4\": Classical explicit Runge-Kutta order 4\n\"IRK4\": two-stage Gauss-Legendre implicit Runge-Kutta order 4\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\ndecision_vars(...; build_state_trajs=true) provides the state trajectories consumed here;\n_register_tracking_system!(...) in trackingmpc.jl chooses this function when the user sets system_kind=:dae;\nuse this for models like ASM2d where the algebraic equations must stay in the prediction model.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\nBuild the time grid and normalize all user-supplied names.\nSplit the MTK equations into two groups: differential equations for stepped states, and algebraic residual equations that must stay equal to zero.\nBuild callable functions for the differential right-hand sides and algebraic residuals.\nRead or infer the JuMP state trajectories x_vars.\nAt each time step, add the chosen time-stepping equations for the differential states.\nAlso enforce the algebraic residuals, either at the nodes only or at the internal RK stages when the method needs them.\nKeep both parts in the model so the prediction stays on the DAE manifold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.register_daesystem"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.register_dmcsystem-Tuple{JuMP.Model, Any, Integer}","page":"Home","text":"register_dmcsystem(model, s, P; kwargs...)\n\nRegister linear DMC prediction constraints in a JuMP model using a numeric step response s.\n\nArguments:\n\nmodel: JuMP model\ns: step response coefficients. A vector means one output. A matrix means size(s) = (N, Ny) where N is the DMC model horizon and Ny is the number of predicted outputs.\nP: prediction horizon length in samples\n\nKeyword arguments:\n\nM: control horizon length in samples. Defaults to P.\ny_meas0: current measured output vector. Defaults to zeros.\nu_hist0: past absolute manipulated-input history of length N - 1, ordered from oldest to newest. Defaults to zeros.\nu_bounds: lower and upper bound tuple for the future manipulated input.\noutput_count: optional output count. If s is a vector and output_count > 1, the same step response is reused for each output column.\nbase_name: prefix for created JuMP variables.\nstore_ext: if true, store the registration in model.ext[:dmc].\n\nReturn a DMCRegistration.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\nConvert the step response s into a dense matrix with one column per output.\nCheck the requested prediction horizon P and control horizon M.\nCreate JuMP variables for future manipulated inputs and predicted outputs.\nFix the current measured outputs and past input history to the supplied values.\nAdd move-difference variables when M > 1.\nFor each prediction step, build the DMC output equation from the stored step response coefficients.\nReturn a registration object that keeps the variables, dimensions, and update helpers together.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.register_dmcsystem"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.register_nlsystem-Tuple{JuMP.Model, ModelingToolkit.System, Symbolics.Num, Vector{Symbolics.Num}}","page":"Home","text":"register_nlsystem(model, sys, obj, ineqs)\n\nRegister a purely algebraic ModelingToolkit system inside a JuMP model.\n\nUse this when the model has no time dynamics. It adds the MTK equations, the inequality constraints, and the objective.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nunlike the ODE and DAE functions, this one does not build trajectories;\nit uses the same MTK-to-JuMP conversion tools;\nit is separate from build_tracking_mpc(...), which is for time-based MPC.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.register_nlsystem"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.resolve_mpc_base_name-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"resolve_mpc_base_name(sym; custom=nothing, suffix=nothing)\n\nReturn the JuMP base name for an MPC variable group.\n\nIf custom is given, use it. Otherwise build the name from sym. If suffix is given, append it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.resolve_mpc_base_name"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.sanitize_mpc_name-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"sanitize_mpc_name(sym)\n\nConvert a ModelingToolkit symbol or label into a safe JuMP name.\n\nExamples:\n\nreactor1₊S_O(t) -> \"reactor1_S_O\"\nclarifier.outlet_stream -> \"clarifier_outlet_stream\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.sanitize_mpc_name"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.seed_mpc_log!-Tuple{MPCLog, AbstractDict, Real}","page":"Home","text":"seed_mpc_log!(logctx, state_values, t0; control_values=...)\n\nStore one initial log entry before the closed-loop run starts.\n\nUse this right before the first simulated control step. It records the initial plant condition so plots and later analysis start from the true initial point instead of from the first callback time.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nmake_mpc_log(...) creates the empty log;\nseed_mpc_log!(...) writes the first row;\nlog_mpc_state!(...) then appends all later rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.seed_mpc_log!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.set_first_move_bounds!-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:JuMP.VariableRef}, Real}","page":"Home","text":"set_first_move_bounds!(controls, previous_value; lower, upper, delta_max)\n\nApply a bound to the first move. Return the (lower_bound, upper_bound) pair that was applied.\n\nThis helper is how the package enforces the idea that the next control move cannot jump too far away from the control value currently applied to the plant.\n\nExample:\n\nif the plant is currently at u = 100;\nand delta_max = 20;\nthen the first optimized move can only lie in [80, 120], after the ordinary lower and upper bounds are also respected.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nMPCControlSpec stores lower, upper, and delta_max;\nprepare_tracking_mpc_step!(...) calls this before every solve;\nsolve_tracking_mpc!(...) therefore always solves with a first move that respects both hard bounds and move-rate limits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.set_first_move_bounds!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.shift_warm_start!-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:JuMP.VariableRef}, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"Home","text":"shift_warm_start!(controls, previous_values; repeat_last=true)\n\nShift a previous optimal sequence by one step and use it as the new warm start.\n\nThis is the standard receding-horizon warm-start strategy. Suppose the previous optimal sequence was [u1, u2, u3, u4]. On the next solve, the first move u1 has already been applied to the plant. So the new warm start becomes something like [u2, u3, u4, u4].\n\nWhy this helps:\n\nthe new optimization problem is usually similar to the last one;\nthe last solution is therefore a good starting guess;\ngood starting guesses often reduce solver work.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nsolve_tracking_mpc!(...) stores the last optimal control sequence;\nprepare_tracking_mpc_step!(...) reads that stored sequence and calls this helper before the next solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.shift_warm_start!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.solve_tracking_mpc!-Tuple{TrackingMPCController, AbstractDict, AbstractDict}","page":"Home","text":"solve_tracking_mpc!(ctrl, state_values, previous_controls; ...)\n\nPrepare and solve one MPC step.\n\nThis is the main online call used inside a closed-loop simulation or callback. For most users, this is the function that \"runs MPC once.\"\n\nWhat it returns:\n\nthe optimized control trajectories;\nthe predicted state or output trajectories;\na small dictionary of scalar metrics;\nthe solver termination status.\n\nWhy it is separated from build_tracking_mpc(...):\n\nbuild_tracking_mpc(...) is the expensive one-time construction step;\nthis function is the repeated online step that uses the already-built model.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\nCall prepare_tracking_mpc_step!(...) to refresh ICs, warm starts, and move bounds.\nSolve the JuMP model.\nCheck the termination status against the accepted MPC statuses.\nRead back the optimized control trajectories.\nRead back the predicted state or output trajectories.\nCompute metrics such as the total objective and its main pieces.\nOptionally print a compact live status line for the user.\nSave the last control sequence so the next MPC step can shift it as a warm start.\n\nIf return_timing=true, the returned named tuple also includes a timing field with a wall-clock breakdown of the online MPC step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.solve_tracking_mpc!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.split_mtk_state_path-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"split_mtk_state_path(var)\n\nSplit a ModelingToolkit-style state name such as reactor1₊S_O(t) into:\n\na subsystem path vector, e.g. [:reactor1]\na terminal state symbol, e.g. :S_O\n\nTop-level states such as x(t) return (Symbol[], :x).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.split_mtk_state_path"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.stage_param_traj-Tuple{TrackingMPCController, Any}","page":"Home","text":"stage_param_traj(ctrl, query)\n\nReturn the fixed stage-parameter trajectory that matches query.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.stage_param_traj"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.state_traj-Tuple{TrackingMPCController, Any}","page":"Home","text":"state_traj(ctrl, query)\n\nReturn the JuMP state trajectory that matches query.\n\nYou can query by MTK symbol, display name, or JuMP base name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.state_traj"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.state_trajectory_base_name-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"state_trajectory_base_name(sys, var)\n\nBuild the JuMP base name for a state trajectory.\n\nNested MTK variables use the subsystem path as a prefix. Top-level variables use the root system name when possible.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.state_trajectory_base_name"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.summarize_dmc_registration-Tuple{DMCRegistration}","page":"Home","text":"summarize_dmc_registration(ctx)\n\nReturn a short summary of a registered DMC block.\n\nThis is useful when you want to check the horizon lengths and array sizes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.summarize_dmc_registration"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.summarize_ic_uniqueness-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"Home","text":"summarize_ic_uniqueness(model)\n\nCount how many first-index trajectory variables have exactly one affine equality constraint and report any duplicates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.summarize_ic_uniqueness"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.summarize_mpc_solve-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"Home","text":"summarize_mpc_solve(model; accepted_statuses=default_mpc_accepted_statuses())\n\nReturn a short solve summary (status, accepted, objective).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.summarize_mpc_solve"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.sync_state_trajectories!-Tuple{AbstractDict, Union{Nothing, AbstractDict}, AbstractDict}","page":"Home","text":"sync_state_trajectories!(c_ic, x_vars, values; set_start=true, fill_horizon=true,\n                         lower_clip=nothing, upper_clip=nothing, missing=:error)\n\nUpdate the initial-condition constraints from values. You can also copy the same value across the whole trajectory as a start value.\n\nThis function is one of the most important online-MPC helpers. The controller already has a JuMP model and already has state trajectories. What changes from one solve to the next is the current plant state. This function copies that newest plant state into the model.\n\nInputs:\n\nc_ic: the dictionary of initial-condition constraints, usually one per state trajectory.\nx_vars: the dictionary of JuMP state trajectories.\nvalues: the newest measured or estimated plant state values.\n\nWhat this function does:\n\nit updates the right-hand side of each IC equality;\nit optionally writes warm-start values into the JuMP state trajectory;\nit can also fill the whole horizon with the current state value, which is a simple and robust default warm start.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nbuild_state_trajs_from_vars!(...) creates the original trajectories and IC constraints;\nprepare_tracking_mpc_step!(...) calls this before every MPC solve;\nsolve_tracking_mpc!(...) depends on this update so the optimization problem starts from the current plant state, not from stale data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.sync_state_trajectories!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.to_num_key_map-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"to_num_key_map(u0_dict)\n\nReturn a copy of u0_dict with all keys converted to Num.\n\nThis is a small compatibility helper for older code paths that may still store initial-condition dictionaries under other symbolic key types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.to_num_key_map"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.update_dmc_state!-Tuple{DMCRegistration}","page":"Home","text":"update_dmc_state!(ctx; y_meas, u_hist)\n\nUpdate the fixed measurement and history variables in an existing DMC block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.update_dmc_state!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.update_stage_parameter!-Tuple{TrackingMPCController, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"update_stage_parameter!(ctrl, sym, values)\n\nUpdate one fixed stage parameter trajectory, such as a disturbance preview.\n\nThis function is how the controller receives a preview that changes from stage to stage across the prediction horizon. Common examples are disturbance previews, feed forecasts, or time-varying prices.\n\nWhy this helper exists:\n\nthe JuMP variables for stage parameters are created once in build_tracking_mpc(...);\neach new MPC solve may need new preview values;\nthis function updates those values without rebuilding the controller.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nbuild_tracking_mpc(...) creates the fixed stage-parameter trajectories;\nthis function overwrites those fixed values;\nsolve_tracking_mpc!(...) then solves with the new preview.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.update_stage_parameter!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.update_tracking_targets!-Tuple{TrackingMPCController}","page":"Home","text":"update_tracking_targets!(ctrl; setpoints=..., lower_soft=..., upper_soft=...)\n\nUpdate the fixed reference values in an existing tracking MPC controller.\n\nThis function changes the optimization targets without rebuilding the whole controller. That is important because rebuilding the JuMP model is usually much more expensive than simply updating fixed reference values.\n\nTypical use cases:\n\nchange the setpoint during a setpoint-tracking study;\nchange a lower soft limit or upper soft limit during a scenario switch;\nkeep the same controller structure but study different operating goals.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nbuild_tracking_mpc(...) creates the fixed JuMP variables that store setpoints and soft limits;\nthis function updates those fixed values in-place;\nthe next call to solve_tracking_mpc!(...) then uses the new targets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.update_tracking_targets!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.warm_start_with_constant!-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:JuMP.VariableRef}, Real}","page":"Home","text":"warm_start_with_constant!(controls, value)\n\nFill every control start value with the same scalar.\n\nThis is the simplest warm-start strategy. It is useful on the very first MPC solve, when no previous optimal sequence exists yet.\n\nHow it connects to other functions:\n\nprepare_tracking_mpc_step!(...) uses this on the first solve;\nlater solves usually switch to shift_warm_start!(...), which reuses the last optimized control sequence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.warm_start_with_constant!"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.worst_residuals-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"Home","text":"worst_residuals(model; topk=20)\n\nReturn the largest affine equality residuals and bound violations after a solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.worst_residuals"},{"category":"method","location":"#EOptInterface.zero_small_values!-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"Home","text":"zero_small_values!(data; atol=1e-6)\n\nSet very small numbers to zero in-place. Works on dictionaries and arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EOptInterface.zero_small_values!"}]
}
